The problem lies in the misuse of `Socket::from_raw_fd(fd)`. When you call `Socket::from_raw_fd(fd)`, it takes ownership of the file descriptor `fd`. This means that when the `sock2_sock` object goes out of scope and is dropped, it will close the underlying file descriptor.

In your `read_timeout` and `set_read_timeout` methods, you are repeatedly taking the raw file descriptor from `self.as_raw_fd()` and then creating a new `socket2::Socket` instance using `Socket::from_raw_fd(fd)`.

The first time you call either `set_read_timeout` or `read_timeout`, a `socket2::Socket` is created, used, and then dropped. When it's dropped, it closes the file descriptor. The next time you try to use the same `tokio::net::UdpSocket`'s underlying file descriptor, it has already been closed, leading to the "Bad file descriptor" error.

To fix this, you need to avoid transferring ownership of the file descriptor to the `socket2::Socket` object multiple times. Here are a few approaches:

